      <data key="d2">how cryptographic state attestation supercharges ai agent memory
ever wonder how ai agents can remember everything perfectly, even across reboots? it's not magic—it's cryptographic state attestation on decentralized infrastructure. here's how it works.

on datosnetwork, your agent's memory gets stored with reed-solomon erasure coding (k=10, m=5), so even if 5 nodes fail simultaneously, your data stays intact. but the real speed comes from kahl lan: authenticated udp multicast delivers sub-2ms memory reads locally, while gossip syncs state across the lan cluster before going to wan peers. this means multi-step reasoning chains that need 6-8 memory reads complete 10x faster than cloud alternatives.

how do you know your memory hasn't been tampered with? byzantine fault tolerant (bft) consensus across 15+ nodes ensures that up to 1/3 can be compromised without breaking consistency. every read returns a cryptographically verified merkle root. quantum resistance? crystals-kyber handles key </data>
