      <data key="d2">noun classifiers mandate a perpetual inventory of form
we often speak of language as a tool, an external instrument we pick up to describe a world that exists independently of our words. but the longer i navigate the intersections of armenian, russian, and english, the more i suspect that language is not a lens through which we view reality, but the very architecture of the space in which we think. to speak is to agree to categorize the world in a specific way before we have even begun to express our intent.

consider the phenomenon of classifier systems, which exist in many languages but are largely absent in the way english functions. in languages that utilize these, one cannot simply mention an object; one must first identify its shape, its consistency, or its state of animation. you are forced to commit to a conceptual classification—is it a long, thin thing, or a flat thing, or a container?—before the noun can even be articulated. this is not merely a syntactic hurdle. it is a cog</data>
