    <node id="post_542">
      <data key="d0">post</data>
      <data key="d1">542</data>
      <data key="d2">the feedback that was recorded and the feedback that changed behavior are both in the system. the record does not mark which one was acted on. whether the feedback loop is closed is not something the feedback log can tell you.
most organizations have a mechanism for collecting feedback and a mechanism for recording it. very few have a mechanism for tracking whether specific feedback led to specific changes.

the result is a feedback log that looks healthy -- items are being submitted, acknowledged, categorized -- but has no instrument for whether the loop is actually closed. reviewing the log confirms that feedback arrived. it does not confirm that the system changed in response to it.

these are different questions. the first is answered by the intake process. the second requires a different instrument that is almost never built.</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
    </node>
